Section: Fixtures

  mk-frame : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  mk-frame (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc else mk-frame (i + 1) n (list-push acc i)

  dev-id-of : PciScanResult -> Integer
  dev-id-of (scan) =
    when e1000-find scan
      is Just (d) -> (d.pci-device-id)
      is None -> 0

  bring-up : PciScanResult -> E1000Device
  bring-up (scan) =
    when e1000-find scan
      is Just (d) -> e1000-init d
      is None -> e1000-absent

Section: Judges

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

  moved : Integer -> Text
  moved (n) = if n == 1 then "yes" else "no"

  k1-read : Integer -> Integer
  k1-read (mmio) =
    let p = e1000-phy-set-page mmio e1000-phy-pcie-pm-page
    in let v = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-phy-pcie-pm-reg
    in let back = e1000-phy-set-page mmio 0
    in v

Section: Entry

 THE LADDER LEADS WITH A KILL AND ALTERNATES: kill, pass, kill, pass. Red's
 condition on the flip, 2026-08-21, and it is why the arm is shaped this way
 rather than the obvious way. Once `e1000-pch-k1-required` ships True the
 obvious arm opens by transmitting successfully, and a ladder that opens with
 two passes is one a bed that never refuses anything can produce. A leading
 kill cannot.

 THE KILL IS A SABOTAGE, NOT THE SHIPPED DEFAULT. Re-running `e1000-link-up`
 resets the PHY, and a PHY reset returns 770.17 to its NVM value, so the part
 lands in exactly the state a driver that never performed the step would leave
 it. The arm producing its own omission is what let the default flip without
 the pair losing a side: nothing here reads `e1000-pch-k1-required` to decide
 what to expect.

 THE SHIPPED STEP IS EVIDENCED BY A REGISTER ROW rather than by a transmit.
 `770.17 as shipped` is 24576 because `e1000-init` performed the step; that is
 a statement about what the driver did, not a ladder state, so it cannot be
 counted as one of the four.

 TRANSMIT IS THE CHANNEL, and receive cannot be used. `e1000_deliver_rx` runs
 on the RCTL and RDT writes of ring setup, before auto-negotiation completes,
 so a frame is in the ring before the stall condition exists and a receive row
 reports frame availability rather than MAC progress.

 THE BOARD DOES NOT POWER UP AT THE BED'S VALUE. Sitting 8 read `0xd104` on
 the ASUS against the model's `0x4000`. Every number below is a bed value and
 must not be quoted as a board one. What the two share is the state of bit 13,
 which is the only bit this step writes.

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let scan = pci-scan-bus 0
    in let did = dev-id-of scan
    in let d = bring-up scan
    in let k1-shipped = k1-read (d.e-mmio)
    in let relink1 = e1000-link-up (d.e-mmio)
    in let tx-killed1 = e1000-send-frame d (mk-frame 0 64 [])
    in let k1-killed1 = k1-read (d.e-mmio)
    in let cfg1 = e1000-k1-configure (d.e-mmio) False
    in let tx-passed1 = e1000-send-frame d (mk-frame 0 64 [])
    in let relink2 = e1000-link-up (d.e-mmio)
    in let tx-killed2 = e1000-send-frame d (mk-frame 0 64 [])
    in let cfg2 = e1000-k1-configure (d.e-mmio) False
    in let tx-passed2 = e1000-send-frame d (mk-frame 0 64 [])
    in act
      print-line-uni ("part is pch        : " & yn (e1000-pch-part did))
      print-line-uni ("shipped k1 step    : " & yn e1000-pch-k1-required)
      print-line-uni ("link up            : " & yn (e1000-has-link d))
      print-line-uni ("770.17 as shipped  : " & show k1-shipped)
      print-line-uni ("1 kill tx          : " & moved tx-killed1)
      print-line-uni ("  770.17           : " & show k1-killed1)
      print-line-uni ("2 pass tx          : " & moved tx-passed1)
      print-line-uni ("  770.17           : " & show cfg1)
      print-line-uni ("3 kill tx          : " & moved tx-killed2)
      print-line-uni ("4 pass tx          : " & moved tx-passed2)
      print-line-uni ("  770.17           : " & show cfg2)
    end
  end

